```cpp
inline const T* get_value (hb_codepoint_t glyph_id, unsigned int num_glyphs) const
{
    if (unlikely (glyph_id >= num_glyphs)) return nullptr;
    return &arrayZ[glyph_id];
}
```

The `num_glyphs` is `driver->num_glyphs` which is `face_->get_num_glyphs()`. The `glyph_id` is `info[mark].codepoint`. 

But wait - what if `mark` is out of bounds for the `info` array? The `info` array has `buffer->len` entries. If `mark >= buffer->len`, then `info[mark]` reads out of bounds. But the crash is a global-buffer-overflow, not a heap-buffer-overflow, so this is unlikely.

Let me look at the `transition` function again more carefully:

```cpp
inline void transition (Driver *driver,
                        const Entry<EntryData> &entry,
                        const HBUINT16 * /*class_table*/) const
{
    unsigned int mark = driver->context_length;
    ...
    const Lookup<GlyphID> &lookup = subs[entry->data.markIndex];
    hb_glyph_info_t *info = buffer->info;
    const GlyphID *replacement = lookup.get_value (info[mark].codepoint, driver->num_glyphs);
    if (replacement)
    {
        info->codepoint = *replacement;
        ...
    }
}
```

The `driver->context_length` is set in the drive function. Let me look at the drive function:

```cpp
inline void drive (Driver *driver) const
{
    ...
    unsigned int context_length = 0;
    ...
    for (unsigned int i = 0; i < count; )
    {
        ...
        driver->context_length = context_length;
        ...
        c->transition (driver, entry, class_table);
        ...
    }
}
```

The `context_length` is set to `context_length` which starts at 0 and is updated based on the entry's `newState` and `contextLength`. If `context_length` is 0, then `mark = 0`, and `info[0]` is valid (assuming `count > 0`).

But what if `context_length` is very large? Then `mark` could be out of bounds for `info`. But `info[mark].codepoint` would be a heap-buffer-overflow, not a global-buffer-overflow.

